window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"20-13","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"20-13-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-13-01/","title_he":"א – 'אל תפנו' בתמונות ובספרים של דתות זרות","title_en":"'Al Tifnu' Regarding Images and Books of Foreign Religions","body_en":"<p>It is a Torah commandment that a person not turn after idolatry (avoda zara), that is, that he not turn to take an interest in and think about its modes of worship, even out of curiosity and without any intention of worshipping it, lest through his interest in it he be drawn after it, or lest his faith and his attachment to God be weakened as a result, as it is stated (Vayikra 19:4): \"Do not turn to the idols.\" Included in the prohibition is not to gaze at statues or pictures of idols (Shabbat 149a), and not to watch their rites, read their books or view other materials of theirs, such as those found on the internet. It follows that one who goes to gaze at idolatrous statues or rites, even if he does so out of mere curiosity, without any intention of checking whether it would be worthwhile for him to worship them, transgresses the prohibition of 'Al tifnu' (\"Do not turn\") (Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 2:2-3). And if he enjoys their beauty, he also transgresses the prohibition of deriving benefit from their adornments (above 10:8).</p><p>Included in the prohibition of 'Al tifnu' is that one may not keep in the house books of a foreign religion, and this includes books of religions that hold a belief in a supreme God combined with other entities (shituf). And although according to most poskim these religions are permitted to the descendants of Noah (above 5:8, 10), since for Israel they are idolatry, a Jew may not read their books or keep them in his home. It seems that books of Islam too should not be kept; even though Islam has no idolatrous corporealization of God, since converting to Islam is as severe as converting to idolatry, so that if a Jew is coerced to convert to Islam he must let himself be killed rather than convert (above 4:7), their law is like that of books of idolatry, as explained in the next halakha regarding heretical views.</p><p>In general, the law of turning to idolatry or to heretical views can be divided into three parts: 1) Turning to and taking an interest in idolatry or heretical views, even out of mere curiosity, is forbidden (as explained in this halakha and the next). 2) Turning to them and delving into them for the sake of understanding the Torah is permitted (below, halakha 3). 3) When one does not turn to them in order to delve into them and think about them, but merely takes an incidental interest as part of broadening one's knowledge of history and the like, it is permitted (below, halakha 5).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Sifra: \"'Do not turn to the idols' – do not turn to worship them. Rabbi Yehuda says: Do not turn even to look at them, and that is enough [to transgress].\" This is also cited in the Yerushalmi, Avoda Zara 3:1. The Rambam (Sefer Ha-mitzvot, negative commandment 10) ruled in accordance with Rabbi Yehuda, that even looking at them out of mere curiosity is forbidden: \"He warned us against inclining after idolatry and against occupying ourselves with its stories… for thinking about these matters and examining those follies is what will stir a person to seek them and their worship… And the language of the Sifra is: If you turn after them, you make them into gods. And there they said: Rabbi Yehuda says, do not turn to look at them. That is to say, even to look at the visible form of the image, or to think about how it was made, is not permitted, so that one not occupy part of one's time with any part of it… And the warning in this very matter, namely the prohibition of thinking about idolatry, has already been repeated, in His statement, may He be exalted: 'Take care lest your heart be seduced, and you turn aside and worship.' That is to say, when your heart delves into thinking about it, this will lead you to turn aside from the straight path and occupy yourself with its worship… And similarly they said (Devarim 12:30): 'And lest you inquire after their gods, saying: How did these nations worship their gods? I too will do likewise.' He warns against asking about the nature of their worship, even if one will not worship them, for all of this leads to error regarding them. And know that one who transgresses this is liable to lashes….\" The Rambam wrote likewise in his halakhot (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 2:2), as did Rav David ben Shmuel d'Estella in Migdal David (on Sefer Ha-mitzvot there). This also emerges from the Yere'im (351), who added that Rabbi Yehuda does not disagree with the Tanna Kamma but adds to him. This is also the view of Rav Nissim Gaon (cited in Moshav Zekenim of the Tosafists on the Torah). According to the Ramban (Vayikra 19:4), by Torah law it is forbidden to turn [to idolatry] in order to consider worshipping it, and by rabbinic law the prohibition applies even when it is out of curiosity.<br>However, Rashi there explained that the prohibition is to turn in order to consider worshipping it, but out of curiosity there is no prohibition. And in Moshav Zekenim (there) they explained that the halakha follows the Tanna Kamma and not Rabbi Yehuda, and testified that this was the practice in the city of the Rashbam. Yet it seems that even Rashi would agree that when there is a danger that turning out of curiosity might draw a person toward foreign views or weaken his faith, he is obligated to refrain from it because of the mitzva of faith, as it is stated (Devarim 4:9): \"Only take heed and guard your soul diligently, lest you forget the things that your eyes have seen, and lest they depart from your heart all the days of your life; and make them known to your children and your children's children.\" And it is stated (Devarim 4:15): \"Guard yourselves very well, for you saw no image on the day that the Lord spoke to you at Ḥorev from the midst of the fire.\" And although the main warning there is not to forget the revelation at Mount Sinai and to beware of corporealization, the Sages learned from this (Berakhot 32b) that there is also a mitzva to guard oneself from physical dangers; all the more so is there a Torah mitzva to guard one's soul from views that would endanger one's faith.<br>Anyone who turns to idols in a way that involves an action is liable to lashes (Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 2:2-3; Ḥinukh 217; Semag, negative commandments 14). For example, if the statue was covered and he uncovered it (Maharshal in his glosses on the Semag), or he went to see it (Mishnat Ḥakhamim on the Rambam, Avoda Zara 11:4). Picking up a book in order to study it is also considered an action (Mishnat Avraham on the Semag there; Yad Peshuta, Avoda Zara 2:2). Some say that even one who reads aloud is liable, since the movement of his lips is an action (Ḥikrei Lev in his novellae on tractate Shevuot 21a)."}]},{"slug":"20-13-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-13-02/","title_he":"ב – לא תתורו","title_en":"Lo Taturu (\"Do Not Stray\")","body_en":"<p>In addition to the prohibition of turning after idols, there is a mitzva that a person not turn his mind to thinking about and taking an interest in evil views that could cause him to uproot one of the fundamentals of the Torah, as it is stated (Bamidbar 15:39): \"And you shall not stray after your heart and after your eyes, after which you go astray.\" The Sages expounded: \"'After your heart' – this is heresy (minut)… 'and after your eyes' – this is licentiousness [sinful thoughts]… 'after which you go astray' – this is thoughts of idolatry\" (Sifrei there; Berakhot 12b). Heretical thought includes taking an interest in and studying heretical ideas, whether in the direction of idolatry or in the direction of doubting faith in God, the special quality (segula) of Israel or the Torah of Israel. As the Rambam wrote (Sefer Ha-mitzvot, negative commandment 47): \"That He warned us not to stray after our hearts to the point that we believe views that are the opposite of the views that the Torah has obligated us to hold.\"</p><p>The intention is not to prevent questions aimed at clarifying the truth, or thinking in the fields of philosophy; rather, a person's intellect has a personal limit and a universal human limit. The personal limit depends on the learner's personal level. The more advanced his stage of Torah study and intellectual development, the better he is able to grapple with views contrary to his faith and to answer them with responses that will strengthen his faith. But if he tries to study these views before reaching the stage at which he can grapple with them, there is a concern that they will harm his faith. Not only that, but even profound words of Torah are not suitable for one who has not yet become accustomed to delving into wisdom and studying the Talmud (Shas) and the poskim. And even then, he should take care to study them with great attentiveness and in light of a rabbi's guidance, so that he not err and understand them improperly and come to a corporealization tending toward idolatry, or to heretical thoughts (Mishna Ḥagiga 2:1).</p><p>The universal human limit stems from the fact that the human intellect is limited, and even if a person becomes very wise in Torah and wisdom, there are realms beyond the grasp of the human intellect, and if he tries to think about them he will ruin his intellect's ability to think properly, and will inevitably arrive at positions of idolatry or heresy. This is what the Sages warned in the Mishna (Ḥagiga 2:1): \"Whoever contemplates four things, it would have been fitting for him not to have come into the world: what is above, what is below, what is before and what is after. And whoever has no regard for the honour of his Maker, it would have been fitting for him not to have come into the world.\"</p><p>These personal and general limitations are meant to give a person the possibility of growing wise in Torah and wisdom without falling into grave errors through occupying himself with matters beyond his grasp. So too the Rambam explained (Guide for the Perplexed 1:32) that just as a person who strains to look at things that he is unable to see, such as the sun, damages his eyesight until he cannot see even the things that he was able to see at first, so too one who tries to think about things that the human intellect is incapable of comprehending damages his ability to grasp what he is capable of understanding. Therefore the Torah warned not to stray after thoughts that could lead to heresy. But its intention is not \"to close the gate of inquiry entirely and to keep the intellect from grasping what can be grasped, as the ignorant and the negligent think, who are glad to turn their deficiency and foolishness into perfection and wisdom, and the perfection and knowledge of others into inferiority and departure from the Torah; 'they make darkness into light and light into darkness.' Rather, the whole intention is to make known that the human intellect has a limit at which it stops.\"<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"In the next halakha we will learn that for the sake of Torah study, and in order to answer a heretic (apikoros), it is permitted to study foreign religions and heretical teachings; consequently 'Lo taturu' applies to taking an interest out of curiosity and not for the sake of Heaven, or to one who has not yet grown wise enough in Torah and wisdom to grapple with these views. This follows all the more so from the study of Ma'aseh Bereshit (the Account of Creation) and Ma'aseh Merkava (the Account of the Chariot), regarding which it is said in Mishna Ḥagiga 2:1: \"One may not expound the laws of forbidden relations (arayot) before three, nor Ma'aseh Bereshit before two, nor the Merkava before one, unless he is a sage who understands on his own.\" To expound arayot means to teach prohibitions of forbidden relations that are not explicit in the Torah; if the rabbi teaches them to three, there is a concern that one of them will not listen and will later be tempted to be lenient about the prohibition. Ma'aseh Bereshit is the wisdom of the creation of the world according to science, or the order of the unfolding of the creation of the worlds according to Kabbala; since these contain profound matters, the rabbi must see that the student has understood them well, and therefore he should not teach them to two. Ma'aseh Merkava is the wisdom of the Divine according to the esoteric teaching, in the manner of the Kabbalists, or according to reason (philosophy), in the manner of the Rambam. Because of the great depth of this wisdom, it may not be taught even to a single student unless the student has delved very deeply into wisdom, to the point that he understands much on his own and the rabbi need only refine and complete his understanding (see the Rambam's Commentary on the Mishna there, Bartenura, Tosafot Yom Tov, and Tiferet Yisrael – Yakhin).<br>In recent generations, through a gradual process, wisdom has increased and many learn from books; therefore rabbis customarily teach even these complex matters to larger groups. Still, the principle remains binding: not to teach complex and abstract matters to one who is incapable of absorbing them and who, as a result of studying them, might come to heresy or idolatry. And there are realms beyond human comprehension, as they said in the Mishna (Ḥagiga 2:1): \"What is above, what is below, what is before and what is after,\" that is, what was before the beginning of everything, and what is beyond all that exists, as the Rambam explained in his Commentary on the Mishna there and in the Guide for the Perplexed 1:31-32. In modern philosophy, Kant established a similar position in his book Critique of Pure Reason, according to which it is impossible to understand a thing as it is in itself, but only as it is received in our consciousness according to our capacity of comprehension. One who understands this principle understands that no scientific discoveries can pose difficulties for faith; on the other hand, there is a mitzva in studying them, since they are the wisdom of the Creator and are included in Ma'aseh Bereshit, which it is a mitzva to study (see Peninei Halakha, Likkutim I, 1:15)."}]},{"slug":"20-13-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-13-03/","title_he":"ג – לשם לימוד תורה מותר","title_en":"Permitted for the Sake of Torah Study","body_en":"<p>The prohibition against thinking about and taking an interest in various religions or in the views of heretics (minim) and deniers applies when this is done out of curiosity; but for one who has already grown wise in Torah and wisdom and wishes to study them in order to deepen his understanding of the Torah, it is permitted, and even a mitzva, to study them. As it is stated (Devarim 18:9): \"You shall not learn to do according to the abominations of those nations,\" and the Sages expounded (Sanhedrin 68a): \"'To do' you may not learn, but you may learn in order to understand and to instruct.\" Moreover, only a sage who had studied the modes of worship of all the idols, and the ways of sorcery and magic, was permitted to be appointed as a judge in the court (beit din), for without studying them he would not be able to rule and judge in their laws (Sanhedrin 17a; Rambam, Hilkhot Sanhedrin 2:1). Even after the destruction of the Second Temple, when the courts no longer judged these laws, the Sages studied the ways of idolatry and sorcery in order to understand the depth of the Torah's guidance (Sanhedrin 68a; below 19:3). Thus the permission to study in order \"to understand and to instruct\" is not only for the sake of deciding halakha in court, but also in order to understand the reason the Torah forbade these things, and to teach this to students according to their understanding.</p><p>Just as it is a mitzva for Torah scholars to study in order to understand and to instruct in matters of idolatry and sorcery, so too it is permitted, and a mitzva, for one who has already grown wise in Torah and wisdom to study the views of heretics, such as heretical philosophical conceptions. For through familiarity with foreign religions and views one can better discern the uniqueness of God's Torah and of the faith of Israel. And those who delve deeply will thereby be able to understand the questions that trouble the people of the generation and the various answers that have been given to them, as well as the advantage of the Torah's guidance, which encompasses all the virtues in wondrous balance and, through the mitzvot, provides an elevating and rectifying response to all the challenges that a person faces. In addition, through studying the various religions and views, the sages will know how to answer the claims that adherents of the various religions, heretics and deniers make against the Torah of Israel, as the Sages said (Avot 2:14): \"Be diligent in studying Torah, and know what to answer a heretic (apikoros).\"<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Kuzari 2:64: \"The Ḥaver said: And the Sanhedrin were commanded that no wisdom among the true, the imaginary and the conventional sciences should be hidden from them, to the point that they knew sorcery and languages. And how could seventy wise elders always be found if the sciences were not widespread and established in the nation, so that when one elder died, another like him would stand in his place?\" The Rambam testified about himself (Letters, Shailat edition, pp. 480-481): \"I have read on all matters of idolatry; it seems to me that there remains no composition in the world on this subject in the Arabic language, translated from other languages, that I have not read, understood its matters and fathomed its intent.\" It was explained that the Rambam studied this in order to understand and to instruct (Or Same'aḥ, Avoda Zara 3:2; Divrei Yirmiyahu and Yad Peshuta, Avoda Zara 2:2), and this is evident from the words of the Rambam himself, that he studied them in order to understand the reasons for the mitzvot. This also emerges from the Meiri, Sanhedrin 90a.<br>Some limited the permission to study books of idolatry and various views in order to understand and to instruct, as the Be'er Sheva wrote (Sanhedrin 100b), that the permission applies only to certain laws regarding which this permission was explicitly stated; and they explained that the Rambam studied additional matters on the basis of the Mishna (Avot 2:14), \"And know what to answer a heretic,\" for otherwise those who err would be led astray after them. Similarly, the Rivash wrote (Responsa 45) that the permission is to study in order to answer a heretic, and added that the Rambam and the Ralbag went too far in this. In recent generations too, some have written that the permission applies only to especially great Torah scholars (Igrot Moshe YD 2:111).<br>In practice, however, throughout the generations many Torah scholars were accustomed to study various religions and views in order to ground their Torah worldview. In their study of the religions, in addition to the justified criticisms of them, they also noted their good points, as the Kuzari (4:23) and the Rambam (Hilkhot Melakhim 11:4) wrote regarding Christianity and Islam, and as our master Rav Kook wrote regarding all the religions, as cited above in chapter 9. All the more so is this the attitude toward the books of the philosophers: although they also contain heretical views, many Torah scholars encouraged studying them (see Guide for the Perplexed 3:29). As the years go by, more and more questions arise from various fields, and consequently there is a greater need for Torah scholars who teach Torah to grapple with them and answer them out of Torah confidence and a broad outlook. Moreover, in these generations, when wisdom has become the possession of the general public, life's challenges demand a higher Torah level, including a certain familiarity with foreign religions and views; consequently, anyone who is accustomed to study and wisdom is included in the permission and the mitzva \"to understand and to instruct.\" See further in the next halakha regarding the educational question."}]},{"slug":"20-13-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-13-04/","title_he":"ד – השאלה החינוכית","title_en":"The Educational Question","body_en":"<p>Although the principles are seemingly clear, in practice there is uncertainty: when is the study of foreign religions and views liable to harm the faith of the one who studies them, so that studying them is forbidden because of 'Al tifnu' and 'Lo taturu'; when is there no concern that it will cause harm, so that there is no prohibition in studying them; and when will studying them enable him to understand the Torah better, or to know what to answer heretics and those who are wavering, so that studying them is included in the mitzva of Torah study?</p><p>This question depends both on the person and on the environment in which he lives. A person of deep understanding and broad knowledge can, through studying foreign religions and views, deepen his understanding of the Torah. By contrast, if his understanding is simple, studying foreign religions and views may confuse his mind, and he will derive no benefit from them. And if there is a concern that studying them will harm his faith, by taking an interest in them he will transgress the prohibitions of 'Al tifnu' and 'Lo taturu'.</p><p>Likewise, one whose faith is firm and whose soul is stable can grapple with foreign religions and views and thereby deepen his understanding of the Torah. But for one whose faith is shaky or whose soul is sensitive, an encounter with foreign religions and views can shake his soul and undermine his faith. Therefore, even if he is a Torah scholar of deep understanding, he should focus on studying Torah in halakha, aggada and musar. And just as it is not good for a person with a sensitive soul to deal with burying the dead, or to counsel those who have become entangled in severe problems of sexual immorality, so too it is not good for his soul to grapple with foreign religions and views; therefore he should engage with them only to the extent necessary for him to understand the Torah and to answer a heretic.</p><p>Beyond a person's level and character, the environment also influences him. Two main factors exert influence: first, the standing of the faith of Israel; second, the degree of exposure to foreign cultures. To elaborate on the first factor: one who is fortunate to live in an environment where the faith of Israel is held in great esteem need be less concerned about the harmful influence of foreign religions and views, and if he wishes to study them in order to grow wise in Torah, he may broaden his knowledge of them with greater freedom. But one who lives in an environment where the faith of Israel is debased, even when his faith is firm and his soul is stable, should be more inclined to put off engaging with foreign religions and views, lest he not find the inner strength to grapple with them. However, sometimes, precisely in difficult periods, the claims that adherents of foreign religions and views make against the Jews trouble many, and then the great rabbis must study their claims in order to equip the public with answers to the accusers and the heretics.</p><p>The second factor: throughout the generations, many Jews lived in a closed society that did not encounter foreign religions and views; consequently these did not challenge Torah learners, those who studied them derived almost no benefit from doing so, and there was also no need to study them in order to know how to answer their claims. Even when Jews lived alongside gentile neighbours who worshipped idols, those neighbours were often not educated, and consequently there was no concern that Jews would be persuaded by their claims. Therefore, the Jews dealt only with the laws of idolatry and not with its ideas. But when the Jewish community was exposed to foreign religions and views, it could not ignore them, and consequently it was necessary to study them in order to answer their claims and to understand the uniqueness and advantage of the faith of Israel. This was generally the case in communities located in capital cities and in centres of commerce and culture. It was the sages of these communities who wrote the important books that grappled with the various religions and views, such as Emunot Ve-de'ot by Rabbi Sa'adia Gaon, the Kuzari by Rabbi Yehuda Halevi and the Guide for the Perplexed by the Rambam.</p><p>On top of these principles, in all generations and places there were rabbis who tended to extend the permission to study various religions and views even to young students, in order to deepen their understanding of the Torah, and there were those who tended to limit it to great Torah scholars, in order to answer heretics. In recent generations, in the wake of secularization, this dispute has intensified. The leaders of the Ḥaredi public tend to protect Judaism through separation and distance from foreign views, and even from Jews who do not espouse this position. By contrast, the greatest of our rabbis hold that in the long term, in order to protect Judaism and increase its influence, one must grapple with modernity and its outlooks, and study foreign views in order to deepen one's understanding of the Torah and to know how to answer a heretic. This was the view of our master Rav Kook.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"The Sages said in the Mishna (Avot 2:14): \"Be diligent in studying Torah, and know what to answer a heretic, and know before Whom you toil.\" Many explained that this guidance includes studying, as needed, books of foreign religions and views, such as philosophy, in order to ground one's worldview and to be able to answer the claims of those who challenge Judaism (Rambam; Meiri; Midrash Shmuel in the name of Rav Moshe Almosnino; Iyun Ya'akov; and others). This is also the view of the Rema (Responsa 7) in his responsum to the Maharshal (cited there, 6). The Rashbatz wrote likewise in Magen Avot 2:14, and explained that the prohibition against reading external books does not apply to books of \"wisdom that follows the way of rational inquiry, but rather to the wisdom that was customarily spoken of at that time in allusive language… But books composed by way of proofs are not included in this, and one who reads them will accept the truth from them and be diligent in learning to answer whatever is against the Torah… And for this reason we have permitted ourselves to read the book of their errors, so that a decisive refutation against them may come forth for us from their own words.\"<br>Some explained that the instruction \"and know what to answer a heretic\" directs one to delve deeply into the Torah, and not to study for this purpose from external books that contain heretical views (Rabbeinu Yona, Avot 2:14; Sforno; Maharshal there; Ḥida in Petaḥ Einayim, Sanhedrin 38b). However, in my humble opinion, they too agree that ideally, one who is suited for it should be well versed in all the religions and views, so that the people of Israel can guide the world; but when Israel was in exile, all that was needed was to survive, and for that purpose they instructed to limit engagement with foreign religions and views as much as possible. It further seems that they too would agree that when there are people in Israel who are drawn to foreign views, one must ensure that there are Torah scholars who can resolve their difficulties and questions. See Peninei Halakha, Likkutim I, 1:16.<br>This discussion is also educational and depends on the person, on the place where he grew up and on his personality, as explained above. It also depends on the educational inclination of the rabbis. From the days of the Rishonim until today, some have held that it is right to forbid the study of foreign views, for fear that people will be drawn after evil views. But the view of the greatest of our rabbis, and likewise the view of our master Rav Kook, is that there is no prohibition for young Torah scholars to study books of idolatry or books of philosophy that contain heretical views. Some of them even encouraged their students to study them, so that they would be able to teach Torah to Israel in a way that stands up against all the winds in the world, and be able to magnify and glorify the Torah and to shine forth the light of the faith of Israel, which will bring redemption to the world. However, they too agree that for sensitive or unlearned people it is right to avoid engagement with foreign religions and views, or at least to minimize it.<br>When one wishes to study various views and religions, it is preferable to study them from books that write about them objectively, without excessive sympathy and without contempt, as the Rashbatz wrote. And in Igrot Moshe (YD 2:53) he wrote, regarding the religions of the Greeks and the Romans, that when the lecturer or the author of the book does not believe in that religion but rather disparages it (as is customary in the university), there is no prohibition. End quote. However, there is no need to disparage the foreign religion in order to remove the prohibition of 'Al tifnu'; rather, the main thing is that there be no concern that this study will weaken one's faith (see the words of our master Rav Kook cited above, chapter 9, note 1)."}]},{"slug":"20-13-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-13-05/","title_he":"ה – לימוד היסטוריה ולימוד אקדמי","title_en":"Studying History and Academic Study","body_en":"<p>Within the framework of studying history, it is permitted to read about various religions that influenced the course of history. For in this way one does not turn to the idols to take an interest in their religion, but rather turns to studying history, and in the course of this learns in general outline about the various religions. As long as the foreign religion is not described in an attractive manner, but only its historical story is described, it is permitted. This includes being permitted to look at pictures of statues in textbooks, and to watch videos that also depict the various rites incidentally. For in this way one does not turn to them or honour them, and there is no concern of being tempted after them; on the other hand, there is educational benefit in becoming familiar with the statues and rites that the Torah forbade.</p><p>Likewise, there is no prohibition of 'Al tifnu' or 'Lo taturu' for one who wishes, incidentally, to look up an encyclopedia entry dealing with a foreign religion or a heretical view in order to complete his understanding of various matters. This is so because he does so briefly and incidentally; because he does so for a general broadening of knowledge that can contribute to his Torah worldview; and because in an encyclopedia entry the foreign religion or heretical view is not presented in a manner liable to attract.</p><p>As for a student who is required to take courses on various religions: on the one hand, the courses are given in a manner that does not encourage joining a foreign religion; on the other hand, he is turning to take an interest in foreign religions. In practice, according to what was explained in the previous halakhot, when there is a concern that the study will harm his faith, he must refrain from it. The same applies to courses in philosophy: if they are liable to harm his faith, he must refrain from studying in them. And when the student has a great need to take these courses, in order to advance in his studies or because of his personal interest in them, he must then intensify his study of books about the faith of Israel and its blessed influence on the world, until it is clear to him that there is no concern that these courses will harm his faith.</p><p>There is no prohibition against reading fantasy books that imagine various magical powers, since the reader knows that it is mere imagination.</p><p>Within the framework of studying art, when it is necessary, it is permitted to look at works of art of idolatrous religions, since in doing so one turns not to the religion but to the art. Moreover, one is not looking at the statue itself but at a photograph of it.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"The prohibition of 'Al tifnu' applies both to turning to the religion out of curiosity and to reading its books, as explained in note 1. However, within the framework of studying history, when the engagement with the various religions is incidental, there is no prohibition of 'Al tifnu', for one is not turning to the religions but to the study of history. Nor is there a concern that this study will weaken faith; on the contrary, to a certain extent it can even strengthen it, when the teachers know how to present the advantages of Judaism. There is, however, uncertainty as to whether, for the sake of accuracy in the historical description, it is right to quote in textbooks sentences from the books of the various religions. Some tend to agree, and some are averse to it. Perhaps it may be suggested that at young ages they should not quote, while at older ages, when the study needs to be more precise, quotations may be used.<br>In Responsa Avnei Yashfeh 1:153, he forbade looking at pictures of ancient statues in history textbooks, because these pictures are the adornment of idolatry. This claim could indeed be made against a Jew who photographs the adornment of idols that are worshipped today, but one who sees the photograph no longer benefits from its adornment, since the photograph was not made as an adornment for worship. All the more so when these idols have become defunct, in which case even photographing them is not forbidden (see above 10:5; 12:8). It is worth adding that in the past all Jews were familiar with idols from what they saw among their gentile neighbours, and thus they understood the Torah's prohibitions, whereas today pictures help one understand the Torah's prohibition of idolatry. See also Aseh Lekha Rav 2:46, who permitted looking at postcards with photographs of churches. Regarding the study of art, Responsa Benei Banim (3:34) wrote that it is permitted to look at an art book that contains a minority of works of a foreign religion, since the learner is occupied with studying the art and there is no concern that he will be drawn after the foreign religion."}]},{"slug":"20-13-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-13-06/","title_he":"ו – ספרי מינות ועבודה זרה של נוכרים ומיסיונרים","title_en":"Heretical and Idolatrous Books of Gentiles and of Missionaries","body_en":"<p>Although a Jew is forbidden to read books of idolatry, one should not damage books of idolatry that are intended for gentiles. This is the law even when they are books of outright idolatry, because Israel has no mitzva to destroy idolatry and its accessories that belong to a gentile (above 3:9, note 7). All the more so one should not damage books of religions that hold shituf, such as those of Christians and Hindus; since according to most poskim gentiles are not prohibited from idolatry involving shituf (above 5:8-10), gentiles are permitted to study them. And when they are elevated through them in ethics and good character traits, there is even a virtue in this for them (above 9:5).</p><p>But if the books were written or sent by missionaries in order to make Jews abandon their religion, they must be destroyed, so as not to leave any name or remembrance of their deeds. Even if the missionaries wrote a Torah scroll, it must be destroyed, so as not to leave any remembrance of the deeds of those who wish to make Israel abandon their religion. This is also brought in the Talmud (Shabbat 116a): \"Rabbi Tarfon said: May I bury my sons (an expression of an oath) – if they (Torah scrolls written by heretics) come into my hands, I will burn them together with the Divine Names in them. For even if a person were pursuing someone to kill him, or a snake were running to bite him, he should enter a house of idolatry and not enter the houses of these, for these know [God] and deny [Him], whereas those do not know and deny.\" And although they contain holy Names, they must be destroyed. \"Rabbi Yishmael said: It is an a fortiori inference: if, in order to make peace between a man and his wife, the Torah said, 'Let My Name, which was written in holiness, be erased in the water,' then these, who cast jealousy, enmity and rivalry between Israel and their Father in Heaven, all the more so. And about them David said (Tehilim 139:21-22): 'Do I not hate those who hate You, O Lord, and do I not contend with those who rise up against You? I hate them with utmost hatred; they have become my enemies.'\"<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"We learned in Shabbat 116a that according to Rabbi Tarfon and Rabbi Yishmael, a Torah scroll written by a heretic (min) is to be burned, and according to Rabbi Yose it is to be stored away (geniza). The halakha is that it is burned, as Rav Naḥman said in Gittin 45b: \"We hold: a Torah scroll written by a heretic is to be burned.\" This is also the ruling of the Rambam (Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-torah 6:8), SA YD 281:1, and the Shakh and Taz there. A 'min' is one who holds a position that endangers Israel and their faith; the Talmud often meant missionaries who tried to convert Jews to Christianity, but halakhically anyone who tries to cause a Jew to convert from his religion is included in the category of min (above 5, note 13; 12, note 3). Ideally, they instructed to destroy the book by burning, which consumes it entirely, but it may also be destroyed by throwing it into the rubbish bin (see Ḥavot Ya'ir 109). This is also what must be done when burning would arouse enmity. All the more so in the case of printed books, for some say that since the printing machine has no missionary intent, they may be placed in the rubbish bin even ab initio (Be-tzel Ha-ḥokhma 3:113:21).<br>The poskim disagree about a Tanakh that Christians printed together with the New Testament for missionary purposes. Some say that its law is like that of missionary books, and they must be burned together with the Tanakh, and there is no difference between whether they were written by hand or printed by machine (Zaken Aharon 170; Gilyon Maharsha, YD 281; and so wrote the rabbis of Izmir, Responsa Shemen Ha-mishḥa 6). Others say that printing has no 'intent', and therefore a printed Tanakh may be used once it is separated from the Christian books (Benei Ḥayei 281; and so wrote the rabbis of Constantinople in Responsa Shemen Ha-mishḥa 6; Maharshak, Shenot Ḥayim, Sefer STaM 42). However, even among those who permit it, some wrote that ideally it is preferable to refrain from studying from them (Ḥida, Ya'ir Ozen 1:79; Maharam Schick OḤ 66; Yeḥaveh Da'at 3:79).<br>The SA (YD 139:15) wrote: \"Books of idolaters in the hands of a Jew – there is one who says that there is no prohibition in selling them.\" Its source is the words of Rabbi Avigdor cited in Shibbolei Ha-leket 2:8. The Rema there added that some forbid selling them to gentiles because of the prohibition of 'before the blind' (lifnei iver) (based on Tosafot and the Rosh), and some say that it is forbidden to sell only to one who will read it, but it is permitted to sell to a merchant who will sell to others, since that is only 'before the before' (lifnei de-lifnei) (Terumat Ha-deshen, Pesakim U-khtavim 27). In practice, the Rema wrote that one who is stringent will be blessed. And the Baḥ and the Shakh (10) wrote that one must be stringent (they were dealing with Christian books from which they studied or sang). See above 11:1-2.<br>However, it must be explained that their words were said at a time when it was very difficult to obtain books, since they were written by hand, and even after the invention of printing, for hundreds of years books were still very expensive. But when printed books are plentiful, the law is as with all objects of worship that the gentile can obtain elsewhere, in which case it was customary, when there is a need, to be lenient and sell to them (above 11:1, note 1). In addition, it seems that they tended to be stringent regarding their books beyond the letter of the law because of heresy, since reading their books roused people to hatred of Israel. Accordingly, today, when many Christians are not roused by them to hatred of Israel (see above 6:8), one should not be stricter regarding their books than the letter of the law. In practice, already several generations ago, Naharei Afarsemon (YD 116) permitted printing Christian books when there is a need. And in Neta Sorek (YD 49), Rav Altmann permitted selling them. So too Masoret Moshe (vol. 4, p. 206) wrote that such printing involves no prohibition of 'before the blind', since even without it there are many books on Christian matters."}]},{"slug":"20-13-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-13-07/","title_he":"ז – תמונות ופסלים שנעשו לנוי","title_en":"Pictures and Statues Made for Decoration","body_en":"<p>It is permitted to look at statues and pictures that were made for decoration, even though the same statues and pictures are sometimes made for idolatry. Some are stringent and hold that if one is not accustomed to seeing these forms of idols, it is forbidden to look at them even if they were made for decoration. But if they are forms that one is constantly accustomed to seeing, such as forms of idols stamped on coins, it is permitted to look at them. And individuals who wished to sanctify themselves were scrupulous not to look even at these (Avoda Zara 50a).</p><p>In practice, the halakha follows the majority of poskim, that the prohibition against looking applies only to statues and pictures that are worshipped, but it is permitted to look at the same forms of idols when they were made for decoration. This is also the general custom (BHL). However, it is not fitting to decorate the home with pictures of idols, even ones made for decoration.<sup>7</sup></p><p>As we learned (above 12:8), it is permitted to look at statues whose worship has ceased from the world, or at fragments of statues found in archaeological excavations. Nor do they involve the prohibition of 'Al tifnu', for since their worship has ceased, there is no concern that a person will be drawn after them.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"In Shabbat 149a: \"The image itself – even on a weekday it is forbidden to look at it, because it is stated: 'Do not turn to the idols.' What is the derivation? Rabbi Ḥanin said: 'Do not turn to [what comes] from your own minds.'\" Tosafot (there, s.v. 've-deyokni') raised a difficulty from the Gemara in Avoda Zara 50a, where it is explained that Rabbi Menaḥem son of Rabbi Simai was called \"the son of holy ones,\" because \"he would not look even at the image on a coin.\" This implies that it is permitted to look at the images on coins, which often had the form of idols, since only the pious refrained from doing so. Tosafot explained that the prohibition against looking applies to images that were made for idolatry, but when those images were made for decoration or as an emblem, such as those made on coins, it is permitted to look at them. This is also the view of the Rambam (Avoda Zara 2:2), the Semag, the Ḥinukh (213), the Rosh, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Beit Yosef 142:15, Knesset Ha-gedola, Shakh 33, and others. Some are stringent and hold that it is forbidden to look at any form that has the capacity to turn a person's heart away from the service of God, even when it was made for decoration, and only when one is accustomed to seeing it constantly, such as a form on coins, is there no prohibition against looking (Rashi, Shabbat there, s.v. 'ketav'; Piskei Rid; Rivevan; Meiri; and Ran 63b in the pages of the Rif). In practice, the halakha follows the lenient view, and this is the general custom (Magen Avraham OḤ 307:23; Eliyahu Rabba 39; BHL s.v. 've-over'; and many others). The Magen Avraham (307:23) added that even according to those who forbid looking, the intention is \"to gaze deeply and contemplate it, but mere seeing – we have no problem with it,\" and many poskim wrote likewise, among them BHL there and Arukh Ha-shulḥan 307:8.<br>Seemingly there is a difficulty with the stringent view: from where did they learn that it is forbidden to look at forms of idols that were made for decoration? After all, in a place where they are not worshipped, it is even permitted to make some of these forms for decoration, such as statues of animals (below 15:1). One may answer that the forbidden forms involved immodesty, since the statues were naked, and through them the artists aroused imaginings of frivolous matters and sinful thoughts (Meiri; Radbaz 4:107), as the SA (OḤ 307:16) says: \"Poems and parables of idle talk and words of desire, such as the book of Immanuel, and likewise books of wars – it is forbidden to read them on Shabbat; and even on a weekday it is forbidden because of 'the seat of scoffers,' and one transgresses 'Do not turn to the idols' – do not turn to [what comes] from your own minds.\" Some say that according to the stringent view the prohibition applies to a full human figure, which, since it is forbidden to make (below 15:1, 3), they were also stringent not to look at (Ḥokhmat Adam 85:9; Divrei Yirmiyahu, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 2:2)."}]},{"slug":"20-13-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-13-08/","title_he":"ח – הקלטות וצילומים של מוזיקה ופולחן","title_en":"Recordings and Videos of Music and Worship","body_en":"<p>One should not listen to or watch recordings of the rites of foreign religions, because one who does so turns to the foreign religion and transgresses the prohibition of 'Al tifnu'. In addition, by listening to or watching them he nullifies the mitzva to destroy their name, as it is stated (Devarim 12:3): \"And you shall destroy their name.\" And it is possible that by playing the recording he also transgresses the prohibition: \"And the name of other gods you shall not mention; it shall not be heard from your mouth\" (Shemot 23:13; below 14:1). But listening to or watching the recording does not involve the prohibition of deriving benefit, since the prohibition of benefit applies to seeing and hearing the rite itself, and not its recording.<sup>8</sup></p><p>It is permitted to watch actors performing the rites of an idolatry that existed in the past and has ceased. For since it has ceased, it is not possible to turn to it and be drawn after it, nor is it possible to honour it, and consequently there is no longer a mitzva to destroy it.</p><p>There is no prohibition against watching a film that deals with secular matters and incidentally shows a religious ceremony, because the viewer wants to see the film and not to turn after a foreign religion. However, when the rite is presented in an attractive manner, to the point that there is a concern that watching it might harm the viewer's faith, he must refrain from watching it, because of the prohibition of 'Al tifnu' (as cited above, halakha 5).</p><p>Some hold that it is not fitting to attend a concert at which a musical work composed for a foreign religion is performed, even if it has no words with religious content (Igrot Moshe YD 2:56; 111). And some forbid listening to these works even in a recording (Aseh Lekha Rav 3:4; Benei Banim 3:35). But it seems halakhically that as long as the work is performed outside a religious framework, and without voicing sentences with religious content, there is no prohibition. For such listening involves no turning to a foreign religion, it does not accord honour to a foreign religion, and it does not cause the name of other gods to be mentioned. And as we will learn in the next halakha, some permit even taking these melodies for prayer; all the more so may one listen to them in a secular setting. And perhaps even those who are stringent not to use them in prayer would be lenient about listening to them in a secular setting. Therefore, it is permitted to listen to classical works by composers such as Bach when they are performed outside a religious framework and no words expressing a foreign religion are said in them.</p><p>However, one who wishes to attend a concert that includes singing must check that the content is not connected to a foreign religion. But verses of Tehilim that were set to music by Christians to be sung in church may be listened to within the framework of a concert unconnected to religious worship, as long as they contain no additions belonging to a foreign religion.</p><p>One who listens on an electronic device to songs or works without understanding the meaning of the words need not be concerned that they might belong to a foreign religion, for as long as he does not know this, his listening involves no turning to, or honouring of, a foreign religion.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"It seems that the prohibition against listening to or watching idolatrous worship is based on the reasons I wrote above, and many agreed with the prohibition itself, even though they did not detail all the prohibitions that I detailed; among them: Igrot Moshe YD 2:56; Benei Banim 3:35; Va-yeshev Ha-yam 2:7; and others. But as regards the prohibition of benefit (above 10:8), there is no prohibition against watching a recording, since the recording is not the rite itself, just as one does not fulfil the obligation of reading the Megilla by hearing a recording of its reading (Peninei Halakha, Zemanim 15:11). So wrote Aseh Lekha Rav 2:46 and Benei Banim 3:34. In order \"to understand and to instruct,\" that is, for the sake of Torah study, it is permitted to listen to and watch recordings of rites (as explained above, halakha 3)."}]},{"slug":"20-13-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-13-09/","title_he":"ט – שימוש במנגינות נוכריות לתפילות","title_en":"Using Gentile Melodies for Prayers","body_en":"<p>According to many, it is forbidden to use a melody that is customarily played in the worship of a foreign religion for prayer in the synagogue, for since it is a melody of a foreign religion, it is repugnant to use it for matters of holiness (Sefer Ḥasidim 238; see above 10:9). One should protest against a cantor (ḥazan) who does this, and if he continues to do so, he is not fit to be a ḥazan (Magen Avraham OḤ 53:31; MB 83). Many are also stringent not to use in prayer the melodies of licentious songs, because thinking about the negative content of the song will impair one's concentration (kavana) (Kaf Ha-ḥayim of Rav Palagi 13:6).</p><p>On the other hand, some permitted using melodies that Christians would sing in churches for the purposes of prayer in the synagogue, since in their view the melody itself was not tainted by being sung in church, and if it arouses kavana, it is good to bring it into the prayer (Rav Yisrael Ḥazan; Ta'alumot Lev; Yabi'a Omer vol. 6, OḤ 7). And some held that melodies of gentiles may be incorporated into prayer only if they also serve secular purposes, but if they are designated for religious worship it is forbidden to incorporate them into prayer (Responsa of the Baḥ, Ha-yeshanot, end of 127). In Arab lands, the cantors customarily took tunes from Arabic songs, even when it was known that their content was immodest, and the rabbis did not protest against them (Aseh Lekha Rav 3:5; Yabi'a Omer vol. 6, OḤ 7).</p><p>In practice, what emerges from the view of most poskim and from the custom of Israel is that cantors should not copy melodies from foreign religions; but if the melody has changed to the point that it sounds like a different melody, there is no prohibition. The same applies to melodies that were found to have originated in idolatry, but whose origin has by now been forgotten, so that they are perceived as ordinary melodies.</p><p>It is permitted to adopt tunes for prayer from secular songs whose content is positive. But one should not adopt tunes from secular songs that contain negative content of frivolity and lust, as long as there are people who know the song's negative content, both out of respect for the prayer and because of the distraction of the worshippers. And when their negative content is not known, it is permitted to adopt them.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"According to many, one should not take tunes from the songs of foreign religions for synagogue prayers, for it is repugnant to use them for the service of God. So wrote Sefer Ḥasidim 238, and likewise Magen Avraham OḤ 53:31; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 53:32; MB 53:82; Responsa Yisa Ish, omission to Hilkhot Tefilla; and others. Some add that this even involves a concern of the prohibition of turning after idols and of honouring idols (Benei Banim 3:35; Va-yeshev Ha-yam 2:7). The Responsa of the Baḥ (end of 127) wrote that one may use in prayer a tune of a foreign religion if it is customarily used also as a tune for songs that are not religious. Agreeing with him were Pri Megadim, Eshel Avraham 53:31; Maḥatzit Ha-shekel 31; MB 82; and others. Regarding the tunes of well-known songs whose content is immodest, the Kaf Ha-ḥayim of Rav Palagi (13:6) wrote that they should not be used for prayer, for thinking about the immodest content of the song impairs kavana. And many agreed with him. Some were very stringent not to take for prayer a song whose origin is not holy (Ma'aseh Roke'aḥ, Hilkhot Tefilla 8:11; Tzitz Eliezer 13:12). Many also wrote, based on the responsa of the Geonim cited in the Rema (OḤ 53:25), that a cantor who sings tunes from foreign religions or licentious songs in prayer should be removed (Magen Avraham 53:31; Pri Megadim, Eshel Avraham 53:31; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 53:32; MB 82).<br>On the other hand, some say that a tune composed for songs of foreign religions, or one that involves licentiousness, has not become forbidden, and it is permitted to use it for the sake of Heaven to arouse kavana in prayer. So wrote at length Rav Yisrael Ḥazan in Responsa Kerakh Shel Romi 1 and in Iyei Ha-yam 152, and he proved that the intention of the Geonim cited by the Rema is that a cantor who himself sings licentious songs in drinking houses should be removed, not a cantor who takes tunes from them for prayer. Rav Ḥayim Konorti, the rabbi of Tiberias, agreed with him. So also wrote Responsa Ta'alumot Lev OḤ vol. 3, Likkutim 11, and Yabi'a Omer vol. 6, OḤ 7 (and in Yeḥaveh Da'at 2:5 he wrote that it is preferable to be stringent). And it was likewise accepted in Arab lands to take melodies from immodest songs of gentiles (Birkei Yosef 560:6; Aseh Lekha Rav 3:5; Yabi'a Omer there). If an old tune was in use and afterwards it became clear to one of the experts that its roots lie in a tune of a foreign religion, there is no need to raise the matter: first, because some permit it ab initio; second, because they did so without knowing the tune's origin. Likewise, if the melody was altered to the point that one can say it is a different melody, there is no prohibition (see Benei Banim 3:35)."}]},{"slug":"20-13-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-13-10/","title_he":"י – מפגשים עם אנשי דת","title_en":"Meetings with Clergy","body_en":"<p>It is permitted for rabbis and public figures to meet with Christian, Hindu or Muslim clergy, and when the aim is to increase understanding and respect between nations and people and to prevent hatred, there is even a virtue in it. So the great rabbis acted throughout the generations, and so did our master Rav Kook, who met respectfully with priests and heads of churches, and so did the Chief Rabbis in the Land of Israel. Some of the great rabbis even conversed at length with priests on matters of religion and ethics and formed bonds of friendship with them, such as Rabbi Moshe Ḥagiz, Rabbi Yehonatan Eybeschutz and Rabbi Yisrael Moshe Ḥazan.</p><p>This was also the path of rabbis of the Rabbinical Council of America (RCA), who participated in interfaith meetings and conferences with Catholic and Protestant Christian religious leaders. However, the great rabbis of the Ḥaredi circle expressed sharp opposition to this. In their words, those who participate in these meetings transgress the severe prohibition of drawing close to idolatry, and the prohibitions of 'mesit' and 'madi'aḥ' (enticing and leading astray). For they claim that the entire aim of the Christians is to cause Jews to change their religion and convert to Christianity, and after seeing that they did not succeed through coercion and murder, they switched to a method of missionizing through propaganda. And through the participation of rabbis in the meetings, Jews will be tempted to listen to them and change their religion (Igrot Moshe YD 3:43; 5:15). Apparently, in their view, the meetings that great rabbis held with priests in the past were for the purpose of intercession (shtadlanut), only to the extent necessary, and therefore they did not lend a hand to missionizing.</p><p>By contrast, the rabbis who were more involved in the modern world held that there is no concern that these meetings will encourage conversion to Christianity. On the contrary, fear of these meetings, like fear of scientific studies and of openness to the modern world, might distance young people further from Judaism and lead them to assimilation. Rav Yosef Dov Soloveitchik also supported this position. It goes without saying that these meetings are suitable only for one whose Jewish worldview is well formed, and for whom there is no concern that he will be influenced by foreign views. In practice, many years have since passed, and reality has proven that these meetings did not cause Jews to convert to Christianity.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"In general, the Ḥaredi rabbis were entirely opposed to public meetings with priests, and used language of harsh controversy against the rabbis who took part in them. So wrote Rav Feinstein in Igrot Moshe YD 3:43; 5:15; and Naḥalat Pinḥas 1:92. On the other hand, in addition to the Rabbinical Council of America and Rav Soloveitchik, who supported such meetings, this was also the view of Rav Naḥum Eliezer Rabinovitch, as testified by his students Rav Jonathan Sacks and Rav Eli Reif. Students of Rav Weinberg, author of the Seridei Esh, also participated in these meetings together with students of Rav Rabinovitch from the rabbinical seminary in London. And so wrote the Chief Rabbi, Rav Bakshi-Doron, in Responsa Beit Av vol. 6, pp. 269-271, that there is value in these meetings for the sake of peace and the reduction of violence.<br>One of the problems with these meetings is that they seemingly involve recognition of the status of foreign religions as equal to Judaism; but the rabbis who supported the meetings held that it is fitting to hold them, because peace is great, and also in order to strengthen and establish the moral agreements between the nations and religions. In an article on the subject of these meetings ('Mi-ma'amakim', in the book Divrei Hagut Ve-ha'arakha, p. 117; the original is in English, in the journal Tradition, 5724), Rav Soloveitchik wrote that the meetings must be based on mutual respect, equal rights and full freedom of speech and dialogue. The discussion should be for the sake of understanding and not debate, and without any expectation that the Jews will compromise on anything in their religion in a way that would be more comfortable for the Christians. He further wrote that one should not engage in the deep matters of faith, which are by their nature intimate and unique to each religion. He also wrote that one should not meet with missionaries who wish to make Jews abandon their religion. Some of his students derived from his words restrictions on the topics of conversation, and some derived that the restrictions are only against compromises or harm to the honour of Judaism."}]},{"slug":"20-13-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-13-11/","title_he":"יא – תפילות משותפות","title_en":"Joint Prayers","body_en":"<p>In prayer, Israel turns to the Lord, the God of their fathers, in a deep and personal way; therefore it is fitting that prayer take place in a congregation of Israel alone, and in the house designated for it, the synagogue. And when a Jew must pray alone, it is fitting that he conduct his prayer quietly and modestly, as befits the deep and personal bond between Israel and God, which is likened to the bond between husband and wife. Similarly, the Sages said that one who displays his prayer in an open and exposed place is considered brazen (see Berakhot 34b; Peninei Halakha, Tefilla 3:8).</p><p>Therefore, ideally, gentiles should not enter the synagogue during prayer. In addition, the entry of gentiles who are not participating in the prayer may disturb the concentration of those praying in the synagogue (see SA OḤ 90:23). However, when there is a need, such as to accompany a sick person or to deal with the synagogue's electrical system, they may enter, as has been the practice for generations (Beit Avi 4:42).</p><p>When gentiles, out of respect and a desire to draw close to the Lord, God of Israel, ask to enter the synagogue or to see the Torah scrolls, it is desirable to accommodate them. Just as in the Temple they would accept an offering from a gentile even if he was an idolater, so too it is right to accommodate any gentile who wishes to draw close to the God of Israel. However, when gentiles wish to enter a synagogue and see the Torah scrolls out of mere curiosity, it is right to avoid this, for the synagogue is a place designated for the bond between Israel and God, and it is not fitting to expose it to strangers. But if this would cause unpleasantness, and all the more so if enmity might develop, one should not prevent them from entering the synagogue.<sup>11</sup></p><p>When necessary, in order to increase peace and faith in God, it is permitted to hold joint prayers with members of different religions, and so have many rabbis who are public leaders done, among them the Chief Rabbis of Israel. However, care must be taken that the prayer be addressed to God alone, without mentioning any idol or person. Likewise, it is forbidden to enter a house of worship of a foreign religion for this purpose. However, since this matter is complex, and the balance between the different values must be maintained, in each and every place the responsibility for agreeing to a joint prayer is entrusted to the rabbis of the locality and the community, and it is they who must decide between the value of peace between the nations and the strengthening of faith in general, and the value of preserving the uniqueness of the faith of Israel and preventing assimilation.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"Sefer Ḥasidim (533) wrote that one should not show a Torah scroll to a gentile, and Yabi'a Omer (vol. 3, YD 15) wrote that in order to prevent enmity it is permitted. And Rav Yosef Messas (Mayim Ḥayim vol. 1, OḤ 13) wrote that when the gentile honours the Torah scroll it is permitted. The SA (OḤ 55:20) wrote that some say that if an \"idolater\" was between a minyan reciting Kaddish or Kedusha and a Jew who hears them, he should not respond, because the idolater interposes between him and the minyan. Some say that according to this opinion, only actual idolatry [i.e. an idol] interposes, but not a gentile who worships idols (Magen Avraham 15; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 22; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 17). And some say that even a gentile who worships idols interposes (Levush 20; Eliyahu Rabba 18; MB 65). Some say that this interposition applies only when the listener is in a different domain, but within the same domain it does not interpose (Responsa Beit David 32; Ben Ish Ḥai, Year 1, Vayeḥi 7), and some say that it interposes even within the same domain (Mekor Ḥayim of the Ḥavot Ya'ir 55:20). Birkat Kohanim should not be cancelled because of the presence of gentiles in the synagogue (Yosef Ometz 70:4; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 128:155; contrary to Zera Emet 3:12). Further on this matter, the SA (OḤ 113:8) wrote that if one is praying and a gentile carrying a cross comes before him, \"and he reaches the place where one bows – he should not bow, even though his heart is directed to Heaven.\""},{"n":12,"en":"In Igrot Moshe (OḤ 2:22) he wrote that there is no prohibition in praying together with gentiles of a different faith when praying with a general text that each one can interpret according to his own faith. But if the text was composed by priests it is forbidden, since it was composed with their faith in mind; however, if the Jew mentions the name of the God of Israel it is permitted, for thereby it is evident that his prayer is to God. On this basis Mareh Ha-bazak (3:112) wrote that it is permitted to hold joint prayers. In practice, official rabbis in various places have participated in joint prayers, and so have the Chief Rabbis of Israel. Likewise, it appears that the Lubavitcher Rebbe supported holding a short prayer at the beginning of the school day in public schools in the United States for the Jewish children studying there, and regretted its cancellation following a petition to the Supreme Court based on the principle of the separation of religion and state (see Minḥat Eliyahu vol. 1, p. 161; Takanot Ha-Rebbe, p. 217)."}]}]});
